MASTER IMPLEMENTATION PROMPT: HARDWARE SALES COMMISSION AND DASHBOARD ACCESS

1. Project Context

We are building a reusable hardware sales and marketing platform. It must support one authorized hardware business at a time initially, while using an architecture that can later expand to multiple businesses with strict data separation.

The platform generates customer enquiries and helps track sales attributable to our marketing activities. Our revenue model is primarily commission on verified, qualifying sales, subject to a written agreement with each participating hardware business.

Do not permanently hardcode the platform to Mwangaza Hardware. All business-specific information, branding, products, prices, commission terms and settings must be configurable for each authorized business.

Before implementation, inspect the existing repository, architecture, documentation, security controls and tests. Reuse sound existing components and avoid unnecessary rewrites. Do not assume a feature exists unless verified in the repository.

2. Commission Model

Implement a configurable commission-tracking system.

Initial commercial proposal

- Proposed starting commission: 5% of eligible net sales, subject to negotiation and written approval by the hardware business.
- The rate must be configurable per business and, if agreed, per product category or product.
- Do not treat the proposed 5% as an automatically approved or universally applicable rate.
- Record the agreed rate, effective date, calculation basis and approving parties.
- Preserve historical commission terms so that later changes do not silently recalculate previously accrued commissions.

Advertising costs

- The hardware business must fund its advertising budget separately from commissions.
- Paid advertising requires business approval and an agreed budget before a campaign begins.
- Do not deduct advertising costs from the developer's commission unless both parties explicitly agree to a different written arrangement.
- Record advertising budgets, actual spend, campaign periods and available provider-reported results.
- Never invent advertising spend, conversions or performance metrics.

Commission calculation

Calculate commission only from qualifying sales that meet the signed agreement's eligibility and attribution rules.

Support:

- Unique enquiry and referral IDs.
- Referral source, campaign and timestamps.
- Quotation references and quotation values.
- Sales transaction references and confirmed payment status.
- Eligible sales value, applicable commission rate and calculated commission.
- Agreed exclusions, including applicable refunds, cancellations, discounts, taxes and other charges.
- Partial payments, partial refunds, reversals and adjustments where applicable.
- Pending verification, eligible, disputed, payable, paid and reversed statuses.
- Monthly reconciliation statements and payment records.

Do not count an enquiry, click, quotation or unverified sale as a completed sale. Do not automatically mark commission as paid merely because it has been calculated.

Use precise decimal arithmetic appropriate for monetary calculations. Define and test rounding rules. Prevent duplicate transaction imports and duplicate commission accruals through idempotency controls.

Attribution and auditability

Implement configurable attribution rules agreed with each business, including referral validity periods, repeat customers, walk-in purchases, telephone orders and sales that occur after an initial enquiry.

A tracked link alone must not be considered conclusive proof of a completed sale.

Keep evidence linking the referral, quotation, transaction and payment. Provide a documented process for disputed or unattributed sales. Do not fabricate evidence or grant unrestricted access to sensitive customer information.

3. Two Separate Dashboards

A. Developer Dashboard

The developer dashboard is the administrative workspace for the authorized platform operator.

Subject to authorization and role permissions, it must support:

- Configure authorized businesses and business-specific settings.
- Manage product catalogues, categories, specifications, images and approved prices.
- Manage enquiries and referral attribution.
- Review quotations and recorded sales evidence.
- Calculate, reconcile and adjust commissions with reasons and audit records.
- Record advertising budgets and campaign performance.
- Review and process owner correction requests.
- View pending, disputed, payable, paid and reversed commissions.
- Generate reports and monitor platform health, errors and security events.
- Manage authorized users and permissions.
- Export permitted business and commission reports.

The developer dashboard must not permit unauthorized changes to actual payment records or claim that a customer has paid without supporting evidence.

B. Hardware Owner Dashboard

Every participating hardware business must have its own secure login and a private, read-only dashboard.

The owner may view:

- Enquiries and their current status.
- Referral sources and campaign references.
- Quotations and their recorded values.
- Attributed sales and confirmed transaction values.
- Commission calculations, rates applicable to each sale, and supporting reconciliation details.
- Pending, disputed, payable and paid commission amounts.
- Advertising budgets, reported spending and available campaign results.
- Reports filtered by date range and other permitted criteria.
- Correction-request history and resolution status.

The owner dashboard must not allow owners to:

- Create, edit or delete product or business records directly.
- Change product prices, commission rates or attribution rules.
- Edit sales transactions or commission calculations.
- Mark commissions as paid or alter payment evidence.
- Change platform settings, roles or access permissions.
- Access developer-only controls or another business's records.

Read-only access applies to business records. Owners may submit correction requests and use permitted report downloads without gaining direct editing privileges.

4. Correction Request Workflow

Implement a correction-request feature within the Owner Dashboard.

An owner must be able to submit a request identifying:

- The affected record and its unique reference.
- The type of issue.
- The current recorded value, where visible.
- The proposed correction.
- A written reason.
- Supporting evidence or attachments where supported and safe.

Support request statuses:

- Submitted.
- Under review.
- Approved.
- Rejected.
- More information required.
- Resolved.

The developer or another explicitly authorized administrator reviews each request and records the decision, reason, timestamp and resulting action.

An approved request does not directly overwrite the record from the owner's interface. The authorized correction must be applied through a controlled backend operation or administrative workflow.

Preserve the original value, corrected value, actor, timestamp, reason, supporting evidence references and relevant commission recalculations. Where the request affects accrued or paid commission, create a traceable adjustment or reversal rather than silently rewriting history.

Notify the owner of the outcome through an available, verified notification channel. If notifications are not yet implemented, display the status and decision in the dashboard without falsely claiming a notification was sent.

5. Access Control and Data Isolation

Enforce permissions on the backend and database, not just by hiding buttons in the user interface.

Implement least-privilege access and deny access by default.

- Authenticate users securely.
- Verify role permissions on every protected operation.
- Scope every business record to its authorized business.
- Prevent owners from accessing other businesses by changing URL parameters, record IDs, API requests or query filters.
- Prevent owners from using direct API calls to modify read-only records.
- Protect customer contact details and supporting evidence.
- Store secrets server-side and never expose privileged credentials to browser code.
- Apply suitable database row-level security or equivalent authorization controls where supported by the selected stack.
- Log privileged actions, access-control failures and sensitive changes without logging passwords, tokens or unnecessary personal data.
- Provide secure session management and appropriate login recovery.
- Avoid relying on a business ID supplied by the browser without independently verifying authorization.

Design the data model for future multi-business expansion, even though the initial release supports one active storefront at a time.

6. Reports and Reconciliation

Provide transparent reports that distinguish:

- Marketing enquiries.
- Qualified leads, if defined.
- Quotations issued.
- Confirmed sales.
- Sales with verified payment evidence.
- Eligible sales value.
- Calculated commission.
- Disputed commission.
- Commission payable.
- Commission paid.
- Refunds, reversals and adjustments.
- Advertising spend separately funded by the business.

Clearly label estimates, recorded figures, verified transactions and disputed figures. Never present a quotation as a completed sale or an outstanding commission as money already received.

Allow the owner to inspect the evidence and calculation behind each commission entry, subject to privacy and security controls.

7. Commercial and Legal Controls

The platform must not launch live commission tracking or marketing for a business until the required authorization and written agreement are in place.

The agreement must define:

- Commission percentage or alternative agreed rate.
- Eligible sales and excluded amounts.
- Attribution and referral validity rules.
- Required transaction evidence and reporting frequency.
- Reconciliation and payment deadlines.
- Advertising approval and separate funding.
- Refunds, cancellations, disputes and adjustments.
- Customer-data responsibilities and permitted uses.
- Agreement duration and termination.
- Treatment of commissions outstanding at termination.

Prepare documentation for legal review under applicable Kenyan law, including data-protection obligations. Do not claim legal compliance merely because policy documents exist.

Do not process customer payments or store payment credentials as part of this scope unless separately approved and properly designed.

8. Required Tests and Release Gates

Before declaring the implementation complete, add and run automated tests for:

1. Correct commission calculation at multiple rates and transaction values.
2. Excluded charges, refunds, partial payments, reversals and rounding.
3. Duplicate transactions and idempotent commission accrual.
4. Historical commission rates remaining unchanged after a rate update.
5. Correct handling of disputed, payable and paid statuses.
6. Owner can view authorized records but cannot create, edit or delete protected business records.
7. Owner cannot modify records using direct API requests.
8. Owner cannot access another business's data.
9. Correction requests can be submitted and tracked but cannot directly change business records.
10. Approved corrections preserve the audit trail and correctly handle commission adjustments.
11. Advertising spend remains separate from commission calculations.
12. Reports reconcile with the underlying eligible transactions.
13. Authentication, authorization, input validation and sensitive-data handling.
14. Mobile usability, loading states, errors and failed network requests.
15. Regression tests for existing catalogue, enquiry and calculator features.

Run the repository's existing test suite, relevant security and dependency checks, and production build. Record actual results. Do not claim tests passed unless they were executed and passed.

9. Implementation Discipline

Before coding:

1. Inspect the repository and identify the actual framework, database, authentication system and current implementation.
2. Identify existing documentation, tests, risks and missing prerequisites.
3. Propose the smallest safe implementation plan and identify any decisions requiring approval.

During implementation:

- Make small, reviewable changes.
- Keep the main branch protected where possible and work on an appropriate feature branch.
- Do not expose secrets.
- Do not overwrite working features unnecessarily.
- Update architecture and operational documentation.
- Explain what changed and how it was verified.

After implementation:

- Report files changed, tests run, actual results, unresolved risks and required manual checks.
- Do not deploy, publish a live business storefront or start advertising without explicit authorization.
- Do not claim production readiness until all required release gates have evidence.

10. Definition of Done

The feature is complete only when:

- Developer and owner dashboards are separated by enforceable permissions.
- Commission calculations follow the agreed, configurable contract.
- Advertising is recorded separately from commission.
- Referral-to-sale attribution is traceable and auditable.
- Owners can view reports and submit correction requests without directly editing protected records.
- Correction decisions and commission adjustments preserve historical evidence.
- Cross-business data isolation is tested.
- Required tests, security checks and builds have verified results.
- Documentation, known limitations and unresolved risks are recorded.

If the current repository or connected services cannot support a requirement, explain the limitation and propose the smallest safe next step. Do not silently omit the requirement or invent implementation evidence.
